    "result_text": "All tests pass. Here's a summary of the change.\n\n## Summary\n\nTelephone numbers are now stored and compared in **E.164 form**.\n\n### Core logic \u2014 `TelephoneNormalizer.java`\nRewrote `normalize`/`comparisonKey` around a shared `toE164` helper that:\n- strips spaces, dashes and brackets (`[\\s()\\-]`);\n- keeps a leading `+` and its country code when present;\n- otherwise assumes country code `+61` and drops a single leading `0` from the national digits;\n- requires **8\u201315 digits** after the `+` (and rejects any leftover non-digit content), returning `null` on failure.\n\n`normalize` throws `InvalidTelephoneException` (\u2192 400) when `toE164` returns null. `comparisonKey` stays lenient \u2014 it returns the E.164 form for duplicate detection, falling back to the stripped characters for legacy stored values that can't form valid E.164, so `CheckOwnerTelephoneUnique` now compares E.164 values.\n\nSo `0412 345 678` \u2192 `+61412345678`, `+64 21 123 456` \u2192 `+6421123456`, and `12` is rejected with 400.\n\n### Entity constraint \u2014 `Owner.java`\nThe telephone field enforced exactly 10 digits (`@Digits(integer=10)` + `@Pattern(\"^[0-9]{10}$\")`), which rejected the `+`-prefixed E.164 string at persist time (the initial 500). Replaced with `@Pattern(\"^\\\\+[0-9]{8,15}$\")` and removed the now-unused `@Digits` import.\n\n### Messaging \u2014 `InvalidTelephoneExceptionHandler.java`\nUpdated the 400 detail message and javadoc from \"exactly 10 digits\" to the E.164 rule.\n\n### Tests\nThe full suite (`AcceptanceTest`, 3 tests including the new E.164 cases) is green. The base `uniqueTelephone()` generator (`04XXXXXXXX`) still yields valid values under the new rule (`+61` + 9 national digits = 11 digits).",
